      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Cohen's guerrilla filmmaking approach gives Q a raw authenticity missing from polished monster movies. Much of it was shot without permits, capturing genuine reactions from unwitting New Yorkers. The stop-motion effects by David Allen, while dated, have a handmade charm that fits the film's rough-edged style. Cohen uses the monster sparingly, focusing instead on the human drama and corrupt city institutions - themes he explored in films like <a href=\"/movie/31579\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"31579\">God Told Me To</a> (1976) and <a href=\"/movie/18502\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"18502\">The Stuff</a> (1985). SUBHEAD: Urban Monster Movies of the 1980s"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Q arrived during a wave of city-set creature features that included <a href=\"/movie/23730\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"23730\">C.H.U.D.</a> (1984), <a href=\"/movie/33518\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"33518\">Alligator</a> (1980), and <a href=\"/movie/10725\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10725\">Wolfen</a> (1981). These films reflected urban anxiety during New York's crime-ridden years, using monsters as metaphors for inner city decay. But while most played it straight, Cohen's film has a darkly comic edge, particularly in scenes of bureaucrats arguing about whether to believe in Aztec gods. The film's influence can be seen in later hybrid horror-comedies like <a href=\"/movie/9362\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"9362\">Tremors</a> (1990) and <a href=\"/movie/8869\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"8869\">Eight Legged Freaks</a> (2002)."
      }
